{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["And the verse's saying <b>When a man has no redeemer</b>: On the path of our Sages of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 93a), אִישׁ (\"a man\") refers to none other than HaShem, as it is written (Exodus 15:3) ״ה׳ אִישׁ מִלְחָמָה\" (HaShem is a man of war). When there is no redeemer for her — for there is no man who sets his heart upon it, none who guides and none who sustains the hand of the congregation of Israel to restore her to her Father — do not say that hope is lost, Heaven forbid. Rather, ״וְהִשִּׂיגָה יָדוֹ וּמָצָא כְּדֵי גְאֻלָּתוֹ״ (\"his hand shall reach and he shall find sufficient for his redemption\"). Our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 98a) that suffering and exile are the rectification of the nation to prepare her, and this is what Scripture alludes to in <b>וְהִשִּׂיגָה יָדוֹ</b>, along the lines of what is said (Deuteronomy 2:15) ״יַד ה׳ הָיְתָה בָּם\" — which is the attribute of Gevurah that strikes the nation in the bitter exile, and through this means he shall attain to find sufficient for the redemption of the House."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:19.268720668Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}